+          <div class="doc-note">
+            <span class="nt">On per-app isolated hosts</span>
+            <p>A deployment that runs in its own measured guest (an SEV-SNP guest per app, or a Hyper-V partition per app) gets the same <code>volumes</code>: each mounted read-only at <code>/models/&lt;name&gt;</code>, the names in <code>ENCLAVE_MODELS</code>, and, where the guest's runtime carries the wasi-nn ggml backend, a GGUF volume preloaded on the CPU as a named graph for <code>load_by_name()</code>, smallest first and only within the deployment's memory. What a name means is pinned in the guest's own measured image: its dm-verity root, so the host only supplies bytes and every block is verified inside the guest; a new model reaches these hosts with a new image release. Hosts fetch volumes from independent replicas and check every chunk against the pinned manifest. A deployment whose volumes a host does not hold, or does not hold verified, is refused before it is claimed, never started without them. The two GPU Shield models keep their own path.</p>
+          </div>
 
           <h4>Secrets: private values that never touch the chain</h4>
           <p>Everything above is <b>public by design</b> - the catalog config and a deployment's config override live on an open ledger, so an S3 key pasted into either is published to the world. Real credentials go in the deployment's <b>secrets</b> instead: env-var-shaped values stored on the platform's API relay (encrypted at rest, never on-chain, capped at 64 keys / 16&nbsp;KB) and handed only to the enclave that holds your deployment's live lease, which injects each one as an environment variable when the app starts - alongside <code>ENCLAVE_CONFIG</code>, so your app just reads its env. Managing them takes no session and no gas: each change is one <code>personal_sign</code> over a canonical string, and the relay honors it only from the deployment's on-chain owner wallet. A running app picks changes up on its next start (<code>enclave restart</code>, or the dashboard's Restart). Plain reality, stated plainly: the relay operator can technically read these values (that is what lets an unattended restart re-inject them) - for credentials even the operator must never see, use <a href="#docs-encvol">encrypted volumes</a>, whose keys derive from your wallet signature client-side.</p>
